<article class="env-corollary" id="822A8A">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/822A8A">Corollary <span data-tag="822A8A">5.4.6.7</span>.</a>overcategory!accessibleaccessible!overcategories Let $\calC $ be an accessible $\infty $-category, and let $p: K \rightarrow \calC $ be a diagram indexed by a small simplicial set $K$. Then the $\infty $-category $\calC _{/p}$ is accessible. </p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Since the map $\calC _{/p} \rightarrow \calC ^{/p}$ is a categorical equivalence, it will suffice to prove that $\calC ^{/p}$ is accessible. We have a pullback diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { \calC ^{/p} \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  \Fun (K \times \Delta ^1, \calC ) \ar [d]^{p} \\ \ast \ar [r]^-{q} &amp;  \Fun (K \times \{ 1\} , \calC ) } \]
</div>
    <p>
       of $\infty $-categories. Since $p$ is a coCartesian fibration, Proposition <a href="/tag/CAB1EC" data-tag="CAB1EC">3.3.1.3</a> implies that this diagram is homotopy Cartesian. According to Proposition <a href="/tag/24A026" data-tag="24A026">5.4.4.3</a>, the $\infty $-categories $\calC ^{K \times \Delta ^1}$ and $\calC ^{K \times \{ 1\}  }$ are accessible. Using Proposition <a href="/tag/5DFB66" data-tag="5DFB66">5.1.2.2</a>, we conclude that for every regular cardinal $\kappa $ such that $\calC $ admits $\kappa $-filtered colimits, $p$ is $\kappa $-continuous; in particular, $p$ is accessible. Corollary <a href="/tag/605E6A" data-tag="605E6A">5.4.3.6</a> implies that $\ast $ is accessible and that $q$ is an accessible functor. Applying Proposition <a href="/tag/950E9C" data-tag="950E9C">5.4.6.6</a>, we deduce that $\calC ^{/p}$ is accessible. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>